practice.solution:
```javascript
function memoize(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return function (arg) {
    if (cache.has(arg)) return cache.get(arg);
    const result = fn.call(this, arg);
    cache.set(arg, result);
    return result;
  };
}
const slowSquare = n => { for (let i = 0; i < 1e7; i++); return n * n; };
const fast = memoize(slowSquare);
fast(9); fast(9);   // second call instant

// Each memoize() call runs its own body, so `cache` is a new Map captured only by that returned function.
```

practice.task:
Fix this class so the button click works, two different ways, and say which you'd use in React and why:

class Panel {
  constructor(el) { this.el = el; this.open = false; el.addEventListener('click', this.toggle); }
  toggle() { this.open = !this.open; this.el.classList.toggle('open', this.open); }
}

practice.solution:
```javascript
// Way 1: bind in constructor
constructor(el) { this.el = el; this.open = false; this.toggle = this.toggle.bind(this); el.addEventListener('click', this.toggle); }

// Way 2: arrow class property (lexical this)
toggle = () => { this.open = !this.open; this.el.classList.toggle('open', this.open); };

// React class components: arrow property is the convention (and lets you removeEventListener with the same reference).
// In function components this whole problem disappears — no `this`; closures over state instead.
```
